{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head eb5673fa689339d75f1b2f10ce4e57bf2cb9ffea (merge base with main 978b8223aa5a736545b3d5cdc57fa415f0de8ca7). Round 2 of my own review: my round-1 verdict 364a915a226bad0b33f2aa042321092affd819d9 requested changes at head 2b4b7b2fd4cd7f79a5f3f38dcda953b3116e0f64 for one blocking defect and left one non-blocking prose note. Both are closed at this head, and I verified the repair by re-running the mutation rather than reading the report.\n\nREPAIR VERIFIED, THE DECISIVE CHECK. The blocker was that the seam this change exists to define was unpinned: in staleness() the term is (world[basis] \u0026\u0026 artifactProvenance) with artifactProvenance := f.isArtifact(basis) \u0026\u0026 f.isArtifact(record.record.ID), and at round 1 the f.isArtifact(basis) conjunct was uncovered — dropping it left the whole package green while, folded over the live log, marking 149 additional live artifacts as describing a superseded world. I applied that exact mutation again in a clean throwaway worktree at this head, artifactProvenance := f.isArtifact(record.record.ID), and the package now fails: \"--- FAIL: TestWorldStalenessStopsAtAReviewReasoningChain (0.00s) fold_test.go:1476: artifact directly answering world-stale reasoning: stale=true world=true\". The mutant is dead, killed by the new assertion and by nothing else. The new fixture is precisely the one-hop case I asked for and not a restatement of the two-hop ones: a \"direct-answer\" artifact resting directly on the \"approval\" report, which is itself world-stale through the seed edge to retired r5, asserting stale=true and describes_superseded_world=false. That pins \"stop at a request, promise or report edge\" as an edge rule instead of a coincidence of fixture depth. Coverage has not regressed: I re-ran three round-1 mutants at this head and all three still die — artifactProvenance := true fails both TestRetiredArtifactMarksDependentsAsDescribingASupersededWorld and TestWorldStalenessStopsAtAReviewReasoningChain; := false and := f.isArtifact(basis) each fail TestRetiredArtifactMarksDependentsAsDescribingASupersededWorld. The mutation was reverted and the worktree left byte-clean (git status --porcelain empty) before the gates were run.\n\nDELTA SCOPE. Nothing in this head exceeds the repair. git diff 2b4b7b2f..eb5673fa is 2 files, 7 insertions, 1 deletion: AGENTS.md one sentence, internal/workroom/fold_test.go five lines. I checked blob identity rather than trusting the diff: internal/workroom/fold.go, cmd/gs/main.go, cmd/gs/succession.go, cmd/gs/main_test.go, SKILL.md and docs/reference/architecture.md are bit-identical between the two heads (fold.go is blob 391cb603e3e81ef4a592707bde420707f38001d5 in both). Round-1's substance is therefore intact and unaltered: the diff against the merge base is round-1's diff plus the repair, 8 files either way.\n\nREPLAY SAFETY. No fold code changed, so round-1's replay proof carries over unchanged and I did not redo the projection comparison. For the record it stands as proved there: no identifier stale or world appears anywhere in the decision region, staleness() is called once from project(), and over the live log at depth 5515 all 5515 decisions were byte-identical on event, verdict and reason, the ordinary stale field byte-identical on all 3751 statements, with describes_superseded_world moving strictly one way (statements 2867 -\u003e 1185, artifacts 704 -\u003e 294, zero false-\u003etrue).\n\nPROSE. The non-blocking note is closed. AGENTS.md step 4 now reads \"The narrower describes_superseded_world fact crosses a direct retired-artifact edge and then follows artifact-to-artifact provenance only\", which is the seed-then-propagate rule the code actually implements — (world[basis] \u0026\u0026 artifactProvenance) || (retiredBasis \u0026\u0026 f.isArtifact(basis)), where the second disjunct seeds any dependent kind, including the approval report that is the main case an agent meets at the merge gate. It now matches the fold.go comment and docs/reference/architecture.md word for word in substance, and SKILL.md discipline 8, which I judged accurate at round 1 and which is unchanged, is consistent with it.\n\nARCHITECTURE: affected layers are the Workroom interpreter (internal/workroom, the pure fold) and the cmd/gs composition over it (merge gate, succession planning, receipts), plus the repository guidance pages. This head changes the staleness contract and documents that change in docs/reference/architecture.md at this same head, with the docs candidate standing here, so the contract change is stated rather than silent and the step 4 condition is met. Layering is untouched: the fold stays pure over records and still verifies no merge head, diff or tree, and the world-staleness judgement remains CLI-side over a folded projection. Round 2 adds no layer contact at all — one test and one sentence.\n\nSECURITY: no defect found, and round 2 changes no attack surface, since the only non-test change is prose. Restating what I verified at round 1 and which still holds bit-for-bit here: the fold's reviewedPaths, which is what actually confers cross-author retirement, never consulted staleness, so nothing widens who may retire whose artifacts; the succession.go filter moves from Stale to DescribesSupersededWorld and so moves toward that unchanged ceiling without passing it, with the other four conditions untouched; signature, ratification, independence, self-review and approved-implementer checks are untouched; the new staleness note is derived from the projection rather than user text and is bounded by the existing stalenessCauseCap of 4 plus an \"and N more\" suffix; unknown body keys cannot smuggle force because validateFields only walks declared constraints and the fold reads merge_approval and the plan fields. The residual risk is the ratified one: an approval whose own review request or promise moved can now land, bounded by the exact-immutable-head checks and recorded in both the git receipt and the durable assertion. Worth stating plainly for whoever merges: this repair does not narrow that, it only proves the narrowing is where the design says it is.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. The repair is the minimum that could close the defect — five lines of fixture reusing the existing reviewRecords helper and the existing artifactByEvent assertion shape, added to the test that already owned this boundary rather than a new test function, and one sentence of prose replacing one sentence. I asked for a test and got a test. One non-blocking observation, deliberately not a blocker and not worth a third round: the AGENTS.md paragraph beginning \"Ordinary staleness in a request\" sits directly under the Simplification bullet with no blank line, so a strict CommonMark renderer folds it into that bullet instead of reading it as a paragraph of step 4. It was placed there at round 1 and I did not flag it then; it changes no meaning, and one blank line can be added by whichever head next touches that region.\n\nGATES, verbatim, run at this exact head in a clean throwaway worktree after the mutation was reverted, with no concurrent go test on the machine. go build ./... -\u003e BUILD OK. go vet ./... -\u003e VET OK. go test ./... -count=1 -\u003e all 25 packages ok, exit code 0: gitseq 0.203s; cmd/gitseq-github 0.208s; cmd/gitseq-mcp 67.350s; cmd/gitseq-perf 0.340s; cmd/gs 173.750s; internal/app 68.425s; internal/connector/github 1.983s; internal/custody 3.254s; internal/docset 113.507s; internal/domain 1.406s; internal/gitstore 4.169s; internal/intent 2.469s; internal/kernel 146.162s; internal/nexus 1.357s; internal/observe 1.632s; internal/perflane 2.326s; internal/perfscenario 2.671s; internal/residentclient 3.052s; internal/service 26.141s; internal/statusview 3.432s; internal/telemetry 3.322s; internal/wireparity 2.388s; internal/workroom 2.477s; spike/cmd/gitseq-report 2.404s; spike/cmd/gitseq-spike 3.843s.\n\nCITED SET. All five candidates stand at eb5673fa689339d75f1b2f10ce4e57bf2cb9ffea and are live, non-stale and not world-stale as I read them before signing: internal/workroom (the primary this review request names), cmd/gs, AGENTS.md, SKILL.md and docs. Together they cover every file this head changes against main.\n\nNOTE FOR WHOEVER MERGES, not a condition of this approval. The live predecessors this merge must retire at step 5 are the current live artifacts at those exact strings, and there are more than one per path in some areas. A separate in-flight head f52630d2 also carries live candidates at AGENTS.md, cmd/gs and SKILL.md; two heads queued at one path collide, because the first merge's retirement stales the second's approval chain. Merge deliberately and check the order.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f2b699c8e51a45a3ec5a8dee84d5c1ee657c2a34","head":"eb5673fa689339d75f1b2f10ce4e57bf2cb9ffea","verdict":"approved"}}